1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,925
Well logging, explained fast.

2
00:00:03,825 --> 00:00:11,637
A tool on a cable reads the rock along the borehole, and every reading becomes a curve against depth. That is the log.

3
00:00:12,637 --> 00:00:18,104
It runs while drilling, after each section before casing, and later through the casing.

4
00:00:19,104 --> 00:00:25,757
Gamma ray counts natural radiation. Shale is hot, clean sand is not, so it finds the reservoir.

5
00:00:26,757 --> 00:00:33,530
SP reads a natural voltage in the mud. It swings away from the shale line opposite permeable beds.

6
00:00:34,530 --> 00:00:41,402
Resistivity: salty water conducts, oil and gas don't. High resistivity means hydrocarbons.

7
00:00:42,402 --> 00:00:48,104
Sonic times a sound pulse along the wall. The slower the sound, the more porous the rock.

8
00:00:49,104 --> 00:00:55,567
Density fires gamma rays into the rock. Denser rock sends fewer back, and that gives porosity.

9
00:00:56,567 --> 00:01:02,288
Neutron counts hydrogen, so it sees the fluid in the pores, and reads low in gas.

10
00:01:03,288 --> 00:01:08,006
Put neutron and density together. Where the curves cross over, that's gas.

11
00:01:09,006 --> 00:01:15,356
Image logs map the borehole wall. Layers and fractures show up as sine waves that give their dip.

12
00:01:16,356 --> 00:01:21,275
Read together, they show where the oil is, how much there is, and where to perforate.

13
00:01:22,425 --> 00:01:25,465
That's well logging: the rock, read from inside.
